{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.75,"content_digest":"a67ab098caed4a4a8c977e35c8df6f8dd08ef865f791968b3992d378106d3b19","created_at":"2026-08-23T13:05:30+00:00","direction":"meta_to_principle","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"f0d4c90a829c02bf9fda1b559a74dfe06efb615cdf93c14eddfbea9efc88b590","input_tokens":5750,"latency_ms":43779,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"07a1a9f4a55189ee5a68f962c49ac4f83037e2081a56a417a8d1e874a8c54fe2","output_tokens":2754,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:078755d384fa775b66352d26","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:unsupervised-identifiability","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:ai-ml:01M0QBJENBMJDKAHXN6D0F1KGN","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The unsupervised-identifiability meta-principle holds that latent factors are not identifiable from observations alone without additional constraints or supervision. The principle's non-identifiability theorem and its resolution by expanding the observation model are consistent with this established result, though the principle is more specific to evaluation-aware adaptation.","relation_type":"consistent_with","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T15:24:41+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0QKJMJD3A13DCG7YJ3ACW1G"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T13:05:30+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
{"condition_compatibility":"compatible","confidence":0.82,"content_digest":"8fc3d4798ae3607420ac3555bd43a32fdf3b79bb0ec1e24b952e880c2fa3089c","created_at":"2026-08-23T11:12:32+00:00","direction":"principle_to_meta","generation_trace":[{"attempts":1,"input_sha256":"53f24acfb4551e339198bbb359937befbefc8c5eea2169958dbb31ecd46d0e46","input_tokens":3899,"latency_ms":29508,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4-Flash","output_sha256":"5c8fdb44a4161e799b453cd923fbd50fe56843d4234b708ac381ad20241ba059","output_tokens":2352,"prompt_sha256":"a0e24249a3438877119c33b7ce1b688152e1189df5765ebd53fae91f7c321069","prompt_template":"meta-grounding-v1","provider":"siliconflow","repair_attempted":false,"schema_valid":true,"transport_attempts":1}],"link_id":"foundation-link:c84f1b5d5980c94d758cab5d","meta_principle_id":"meta:ai-ml:inductive-bias","meta_principle_revision":1,"principle_id":"prn:chemistry-materials:01M0Q541CN04EWBGZ0BHY853H0","principle_revision":1,"rationale":"The claim that abstract-only pretraining makes embeddings more sensitive to compositional trends than to processing-dependent structural variations is a domain-specific instance of the general inductive-bias principle: finite training data and corpus choice bias a learner toward certain representations. The abstract corpus lacks detailed processing histories, so the learned embeddings preferentially capture compositional regularities, exactly the kind of bias the meta-principle describes.","relation_type":"specializes","review_attestation":{"actor":"principia-owner","basis":"Explicit Submit reviewed changeset confirmation in local Admin","reviewed_at":"2026-08-23T15:24:41+00:00","source_review_status":"unassessed","trace_id":"sync:01M0QKJMJD3A13DCG7YJ3ACW1G"},"review_status":"reviewed","revision":1,"scale_compatibility":"compatible","schema_version":"global-foundation-link-v1","status":"active","updated_at":"2026-08-23T11:12:32+00:00","variable_compatibility":"compatible"}
